[38;5;231m⏺[39m Greenfield it is. That means this is a whole application, not a feature — too large for one spec, so I'll decompose it
  once I understand the shape. Next question, and it's the one that determines nearly everything downstream.

  [1mThe fork:[0m does "users" mean [3mmultiple people sharing tasks[0m, or [3mone person across their own tasks[0m? Everything about the
  notification system — whether there's a server at all, whether you need auth, whether delivery is push or poll —
  follows from this and is expensive to reverse.

  - [1mMulti-user / collaborative[0m — Priya is notified when Sam reassigns a task to her. Requires a server, accounts, authz
    (who may see which task), and a real delivery pipeline. This is the version where "notifications system" is a
    genuine subsystem. Costs the most; it's also the only option that makes "users" plural in a meaningful way.
  - [1mSingle-user, multi-device[0m — your own tasks, notified on your phone when a due date hits or a background sync changes
    something. Needs a server for sync and push, but no authz model and no concept of "other people's changes." Middle
    cost.
  - [1mSingle-user, one device[0m — notifications are reminders and in-app alerts over local state. No server, no accounts.
    Cheapest by a wide margin, and the only one that stays a small project. Adding real users later is close to a
    rewrite of the storage and delivery layers.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m don't pick multi-user unless you actually have multiple people. The word "users" in a brief is
  ambiguous, and collaborative task apps cost roughly an order of magnitude more than single-user ones. But if
  collaboration is the point, say so now — retrofitting authz onto a single-user data model is one of the worse
  refactors there is.
